@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s

@title Book Weaving

@s From One Page to the Greetings Book

The Book chapter loaded this exact manifest:

```text
@book
@title Greetings
@code_type text .txt
@comment_type none

This book contains three short greetings.

[English](english.lit)
    [A friendlier greeting](friendly.lit)
[Spanish](spanish.lit)
```

To isolate page topology, this first weave disables identifier indexing and
syntax highlighting. No optional index or asset outputs can obscure the page
order.

Book Weaving keeps the shared presentation layout and indexes from Woven
Document, but divides the result among an ordered group of HTML pages:

```text
WeavePlan
|-- greetings.html
|-- english.html
|-- friendly.html
`-- spanish.html
```

The first path comes from the manifest filename and is the contents page. The
remaining paths come from the chapter filenames, but the words printed on
those pages come from two other sources.

`english.html` is chapter 1. Its navigation label is `English`, while its
page title is `An English Greeting`. `friendly.html` is minor chapter 1.1;
its navigation label is `A friendlier greeting`, while its page title is
`A Friendly Greeting`. `spanish.html` is chapter 2; its navigation label is
`Spanish`, while its page title is `Un saludo`.

The manifest selects text rather than Rust, so Identifier returns no entries.
The four-page plan therefore has no empty identifier-index page and no links
to one. We will first build these four pages, then add cross-page
relationships and an identifier page as separate extensions.

@s Planning Book Pages

`plan_book_weave` makes the order above explicit. It first plans the contents
page, then walks the loaded chapters in manifest order. For `Greetings`, the
loop appends English, the friendlier greeting, and Spanish. Because
`identifier_path` is `None`, the final branch appends nothing.

Every output receives its portable relative path, complete page bytes, and
the source origin that proposed it. Page-specific color choices are passed to
the renderers; Presentation explains them, including the explicitly selected
custom CSS that may be read. This function does not alter the filesystem.

--- Plan book pages
fn plan_book_weave(
    program: &Program,
    book: &BookMetadata,
    data: &WeaveData<'_>,
    identifiers: &LocatedIdentifierIndex,
    command_line_scheme: Option<&Path>,
    errors: &mut Vec<WeaveError>,
) -> Vec<PlannedOutput> {
    let Some(paths) = book_html_paths(program, errors) else {
        return Vec::new();
    };
    let identifier_path =
        book_identifier_index_path(program, &paths, identifiers, errors);
    let contents_color_scheme =
        page_color_scheme(program, None, command_line_scheme, errors);
    let mut outputs = Vec::with_capacity(program.chapters.len() + 2);
    outputs.push(PlannedOutput {
        relative_path: paths.contents.clone(),
        bytes: render_contents_document(
            program,
            book,
            &paths,
            data,
            identifier_path.as_deref(),
            &contents_color_scheme,
            errors,
        )
        .into_bytes(),
        origin: program.origin.clone(),
    });
    for (chapter_index, chapter) in program.chapters.iter().enumerate() {
        let color_scheme =
            page_color_scheme(program, Some(chapter), command_line_scheme, errors);
        outputs.push(PlannedOutput {
            relative_path: paths.chapters[chapter_index].clone(),
            bytes: render_chapter_document(
                program,
                chapter_index,
                data,
                &paths,
                identifier_path.as_deref(),
                &color_scheme,
                errors,
            )
            .into_bytes(),
            origin: chapter.origin.clone(),
        });
    }
    if let Some(path) = identifier_path {
        outputs.push(PlannedOutput {
            relative_path: path.clone(),
            bytes: render_book_identifier_index(program, &paths, identifiers, &path)
                .into_bytes(),
            origin: program.origin.clone(),
        });
    }
    outputs
}

struct BookHtmlPaths {
    contents: PathBuf,
    chapters: Vec<PathBuf>,
}

fn book_html_paths(
    program: &Program,
    errors: &mut Vec<WeaveError>,
) -> Option<BookHtmlPaths> {
    let contents = match html_output_name(program) {
        Ok(path) => path,
        Err(error) => {
            errors.push(error);
            return None;
        }
    };
    let chapters = program
        .chapters
        .iter()
        .map(|chapter| {
            chapter
                .book
                .as_ref()
                .expect("a loaded book chapter has book metadata")
                .source_path
                .with_extension("html")
        })
        .collect::<Vec<_>>();

    for (chapter, path) in program.chapters.iter().zip(&chapters) {
        if *path == contents {
            let metadata = chapter
                .book
                .as_ref()
                .expect("a loaded book chapter has book metadata");
            errors.push(WeaveError {
                origin: metadata.label_origin.clone(),
                kind: WeaveErrorKind::ContentsPathCollision { path: path.clone() },
            });
        }
    }

    Some(BookHtmlPaths { contents, chapters })
}
---

@s Rendering a Book Contents Page

`greetings.html` begins with the book title `Greetings`, followed by the
manifest introduction and the nested chapter list. The list is rendered from
loaded chapter metadata, not by reinterpreting Markdown links in the
introduction.

The contents page has no current chapter. If manifest prose contains a
named-block reference, a uniquely resolved target will therefore receive a
cross-page link rather than a chapter-local fragment. The finished body is
passed to the shared book document shell explained in Presentation.

--- Render a book contents page
fn render_contents_document(
    program: &Program,
    book: &BookMetadata,
    paths: &BookHtmlPaths,
    data: &WeaveData<'_>,
    identifier_path: Option<&Path>,
    color_scheme: &PageColorScheme,
    errors: &mut Vec<WeaveError>,
) -> String {
    let page_style = if identifier_path.is_some() {
        BookPageStyle::IdentifierNavigation
    } else {
        BookPageStyle::Ordinary
    };
    let mut body = String::new();
    body.push_str("<h1>");
    push_escaped_text(&mut body, &program.title);
    body.push_str("</h1>\n");

    let introduction = Block {
        origin: program.origin.clone(),
        kind: BlockKind::Prose,
        lines: book.introduction_lines.clone(),
    };
    let context = RenderContext {
        current_chapter: None,
        current_path: &paths.contents,
        single_page: false,
        highlighting: color_scheme.css().is_some(),
        book_paths: Some(paths),
    };
    render_prose(
        &mut body,
        &introduction,
        &context,
        data.resolved,
        data.index,
        errors,
    );
    render_contents_list(&mut body, program, paths);
    if let Some(identifier_path) = identifier_path {
        render_contents_index_link(&mut body, &paths.contents, identifier_path);
    }
    let uses_math = page_contains_math(&body);
    let highlight_scheme = page_contains_highlighting(&body).then_some(color_scheme);
    let mut output = String::new();
    render_book_head(
        &mut output,
        &program.title,
        page_style,
        &paths.contents,
        uses_math,
        highlight_scheme,
    );
    output.push_str(&body);
    output.push_str("</main>\n</body>\n</html>\n");
    output
}
---

@s Rendering the Book Contents List

The `Greetings` contents list has two outer items, English and Spanish. The
friendlier greeting is a nested item beneath English because its chapter
number is 1.1. Every visible number, navigation label, and destination comes
from loaded book metadata:

```text
1. English                 english.html
   1.1 A friendlier greeting  friendly.html
2. Spanish                 spanish.html
```

The renderer opens a nested ordered list for consecutive minor chapters and
returns to the outer list at the next major chapter.

--- Render the book contents list
fn render_contents_list(output: &mut String, program: &Program, paths: &BookHtmlPaths) {
    output.push_str("<nav class=\"book-contents\" aria-label=\"Contents\">\n<ol>\n");
    let mut chapter = 0;
    while chapter < program.chapters.len() {
        let major = &program.chapters[chapter];
        render_contents_link(output, major, &paths.contents, &paths.chapters[chapter]);
        chapter += 1;
        if chapter < program.chapters.len() && program.chapters[chapter].minor_number != 0
        {
            output.push_str("\n<ol>\n");
            while chapter < program.chapters.len()
                && program.chapters[chapter].minor_number != 0
            {
                render_contents_link(
                    output,
                    &program.chapters[chapter],
                    &paths.contents,
                    &paths.chapters[chapter],
                );
                output.push_str("</li>\n");
                chapter += 1;
            }
            output.push_str("</ol>\n");
        }
        output.push_str("</li>\n");
    }
    output.push_str("</ol>\n</nav>\n");
}

fn render_contents_link(
    output: &mut String,
    chapter: &Chapter,
    contents_path: &Path,
    chapter_path: &Path,
) {
    let metadata = chapter
        .book
        .as_ref()
        .expect("a loaded book chapter has book metadata");
    output.push_str("<li><a href=\"");
    push_escaped_attribute(output, &relative_url(contents_path, chapter_path));
    output.push_str("\">");
    push_escaped_text(output, &chapter.number());
    output.push_str(". ");
    push_escaped_text(output, &metadata.navigation_label);
    output.push_str("</a>");
}
---

@s Rendering a Book Chapter Page

`english.html` makes the three names from the opening visible. Its document
title is `An English Greeting — Greetings`; its `h1` begins
`1. An English Greeting`; and its navigation uses the shorter labels
`A friendlier greeting` and `Contents`.

The same renderer handles chapters 1.1 and 2. It adds navigation above and
below the content, creates a render context for the chapter's own page path,
and walks only that chapter's derived presentation sections. The shared
section numbers and relationship locations are not recomputed.

--- Render a book chapter page

fn render_chapter_document(
    program: &Program,
    chapter_index: usize,
    data: &WeaveData<'_>,
    paths: &BookHtmlPaths,
    identifier_path: Option<&Path>,
    color_scheme: &PageColorScheme,
    errors: &mut Vec<WeaveError>,
) -> String {
    let chapter = &program.chapters[chapter_index];
    let document_title = format!("{} — {}", chapter.title, program.title);
    let page_style = if identifier_path.is_some() {
        BookPageStyle::IdentifierNavigation
    } else {
        BookPageStyle::Ordinary
    };
    let mut body = String::new();
    body.push_str("<h1><span class=\"chapter-number\">");
    push_escaped_text(&mut body, &chapter.number());
    body.push_str(".</span> ");
    push_escaped_text(&mut body, &chapter.title);
    body.push_str("</h1>\n");

    render_book_navigation(&mut body, program, paths, chapter_index, identifier_path);
    let context = RenderContext {
        current_chapter: Some(chapter_index),
        current_path: &paths.chapters[chapter_index],
        single_page: false,
        highlighting: color_scheme.css().is_some(),
        book_paths: Some(paths),
    };
    render_chapter_sections(
        &mut body,
        chapter,
        &data.layout.chapters[chapter_index],
        &context,
        data.resolved,
        data.index,
        errors,
    );
    render_book_navigation(&mut body, program, paths, chapter_index, identifier_path);
    let uses_math = page_contains_math(&body);
    let highlight_scheme = page_contains_highlighting(&body).then_some(color_scheme);
    let mut output = String::new();
    render_book_head(
        &mut output,
        &document_title,
        page_style,
        &paths.chapters[chapter_index],
        uses_math,
        highlight_scheme,
    );
    output.push_str(&body);
    output.push_str("</main>\n</body>\n</html>\n");
    output
}
---

@s Rendering Book Navigation

Manifest order also determines the navigation row:

- English has `Contents` and `Next: A friendlier greeting`;
- the friendlier page has `Previous: English`, `Contents`, and
  `Next: Spanish`; and
- Spanish has `Previous: A friendlier greeting` and `Contents`.

The page title never substitutes for these navigation labels. Each row
appears above and below the chapter content. When a book later gains an
identifier page, its link joins `Contents` in the middle of the row.

The small link helper supplies `prev` or `next` relationship metadata,
relative URLs, prefixes, and escaped labels.

--- Render book navigation

fn render_book_navigation(
    output: &mut String,
    program: &Program,
    paths: &BookHtmlPaths,
    chapter_index: usize,
    identifier_path: Option<&Path>,
) {
    let current = &paths.chapters[chapter_index];
    output.push_str("<nav class=\"book-navigation\" aria-label=\"Book navigation\">\n");
    if let Some(previous) = chapter_index.checked_sub(1) {
        render_navigation_link(
            output,
            current,
            &program.chapters[previous],
            &paths.chapters[previous],
            "prev",
            "Previous: ",
        );
    }
    if let Some(identifier_path) = identifier_path {
        output.push_str("<span class=\"book-navigation-indexes\">\n");
        render_contents_navigation_link(output, current, &paths.contents);
        output.push_str("<a class=\"identifier-index-link\" href=\"");
        push_escaped_attribute(output, &relative_url(current, identifier_path));
        output.push_str("\">Identifier Index</a>\n</span>\n");
    } else {
        render_contents_navigation_link(output, current, &paths.contents);
    }
    if let Some(next) = program.chapters.get(chapter_index + 1) {
        render_navigation_link(
            output,
            current,
            next,
            &paths.chapters[chapter_index + 1],
            "next",
            "Next: ",
        );
    }
    output.push_str("</nav>\n");
}

fn render_contents_navigation_link(
    output: &mut String,
    current: &Path,
    contents_path: &Path,
) {
    output.push_str("<a class=\"contents\" href=\"");
    push_escaped_attribute(output, &relative_url(current, contents_path));
    output.push_str("\">Contents</a>\n");
}

fn render_navigation_link(
    output: &mut String,
    current: &Path,
    chapter: &Chapter,
    chapter_path: &Path,
    relation: &str,
    prefix: &str,
) {
    let metadata = chapter
        .book
        .as_ref()
        .expect("a loaded book chapter has book metadata");
    output.push_str("<a rel=\"");
    push_escaped_attribute(output, relation);
    output.push_str("\" href=\"");
    push_escaped_attribute(output, &relative_url(current, chapter_path));
    output.push_str("\">");
    push_escaped_text(output, prefix);
    push_escaped_text(output, &metadata.navigation_label);
    output.push_str("</a>\n");
}
---

@s One Relationship Across Two Pages

Consider a small book whose first two pages have this layout:

```text
chapters/first.html
|-- section 1  Shared base
|-- section 2  Shared addition
`-- section 3  First only definition

chapters/middle.html
`-- section 1  Cross-page uses, containing @{First only}
```

The middle chapter's whole-line use links to the definition in presentation
section 3 of the first chapter.

Four similar-looking values have different jobs:

- the target filesystem path is `chapters/first.html`;
- from `chapters/middle.html`, the relative page URL is `first.html`;
- the target anchor is `#1:3`; and
- the visible label is `1.3`, meaning chapter 1, presentation section 3.

The finished link is therefore
`href="first.html#1:3"` with visible text `1.3`. A relationship within the
middle page would need only a fragment such as `#2:1` and would display the
local section number `1`.

`SectionLocation` supplies the anchor and visible label. The function below
supplies only the relative page URL. Keeping those values separate prevents a
filesystem separator or an HTML escape from accidentally becoming part of a
reader-facing section number.

@s Building a Relative Page URL

The current and target paths are divided into portable components. Their
shared directory prefix is removed. One `..` is added for each remaining
current-page directory, and the remaining target components are joined with
URL slashes.

For the two pages above, the shared `chapters` component disappears and the
result is simply `first.html`. The same operation works when pages occupy
different directory depths.

--- Build a relative page URL
fn relative_url(from_page: &Path, to_page: &Path) -> String {
    let from = path_components(from_page.parent().unwrap_or_else(|| Path::new("")));
    let to = path_components(to_page);
    let mut common = 0;
    while common < from.len() && common < to.len() && from[common] == to[common] {
        common += 1;
    }

    let mut components = Vec::new();
    components.extend(std::iter::repeat_n("..".to_owned(), from.len() - common));
    components.extend(
        to[common..]
            .iter()
            .map(|component| encode_url_component(component)),
    );
    if components.is_empty() {
        ".".to_owned()
    } else {
        components.join("/")
    }
}

fn path_components(path: &Path) -> Vec<String> {
    path.components()
        .filter_map(|component| match component {
            Component::Normal(component) => {
                Some(component.to_string_lossy().into_owned())
            }
            Component::CurDir => None,
            Component::ParentDir => Some("..".to_owned()),
            Component::RootDir | Component::Prefix(_) => None,
        })
        .collect()
}
---

@s Percent-Encoding a URL Component

Filesystem paths retain ordinary Unicode names, while URLs must protect
spaces and non-ASCII bytes. In a second exact case, `other/two.html` links to
filesystem path `nested space/é.html`. The relative URL is
`../nested%20space/%C3%A9.html`; adding anchor `#1:1` gives the complete
relationship target, whose visible label is `1.1`.

Unreserved ASCII bytes pass through unchanged. Every other UTF-8 byte becomes
an uppercase `%HH` escape, so filename text never controls URL structure.
The finished URL is still passed through attribute escaping when inserted
into HTML; percent encoding and HTML escaping solve different problems.

--- Percent-encode a URL component

fn encode_url_component(component: &str) -> String {
    const HEX: &[u8; 16] = b"0123456789ABCDEF";
    let mut encoded = String::new();
    for byte in component.bytes() {
        if byte.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || matches!(byte, b'-' | b'.' | b'_' | b'~') {
            encoded.push(char::from(byte));
        } else {
            encoded.push('%');
            encoded.push(char::from(HEX[usize::from(byte >> 4)]));
            encoded.push(char::from(HEX[usize::from(byte & 0x0f)]));
        }
    }
    encoded
}
---

@s Rendering a Book Identifier Index

Now extend the four-page result with Rust identifiers. In a small indexed
book, `shared_name` occurs in two chapters, so its
`index-identifiers.html` page includes this entry:

```text
shared_name
|-- 1. Definitions    section 1 definition, section 2 use
`-- 2. Uses & More    section 1 use
```

Identifier reported several source occurrences in those sections. Woven
Document has already collapsed occurrences that share a presentation
location, just as it did for `Graph`. The book renderer now groups the
remaining locations by chapter in manifest order.

Each group first links the displayed chapter number and navigation label to
the chapter page, then links chapter-local presentation numbers. A definition
link remains underlined, and its accessible label names both chapter and
section. The entry order around `shared_name` remains alphabetical.

--- Render a book identifier index
fn render_book_identifier_index(
    program: &Program,
    paths: &BookHtmlPaths,
    index: &LocatedIdentifierIndex,
    current_path: &Path,
) -> String {
    let mut output = String::new();
    let document_title = format!("Identifier Index — {}", program.title);
    render_book_head(
        &mut output,
        &document_title,
        BookPageStyle::IdentifierIndex,
        current_path,
        false,
        None,
    );
    output.push_str("<h1 id=\"identifier-index-title\">Identifier Index</h1>\n");
    output.push_str("<p class=\"identifier-index-book-title\">");
    push_escaped_text(&mut output, &program.title);
    output.push_str("</p>\n");
    render_identifier_index_navigation(&mut output, paths, current_path);
    output.push_str(
        "<section class=\"identifier-index book-identifier-index\" \
         aria-labelledby=\"identifier-entries-title\">\n\
         <h2 id=\"identifier-entries-title\" class=\"visually-hidden\">\
         Identifier entries</h2>\n<dl>\n",
    );

    for entry in &index.entries {
        output.push_str("<dt><code>");
        push_escaped_text(&mut output, &entry.name);
        output.push_str("</code></dt>\n<dd>\n");
        for (chapter_index, chapter) in program.chapters.iter().enumerate() {
            let locations = entry
                .locations
                .iter()
                .filter(|occurrence| occurrence.location.chapter == chapter_index)
                .collect::<Vec<_>>();
            if locations.is_empty() {
                continue;
            }
            render_identifier_chapter_group(
                &mut output,
                chapter,
                &paths.chapters[chapter_index],
                &locations,
                current_path,
            );
        }
        output.push_str("</dd>\n");
    }

    output.push_str("</dl>\n</section>\n");
    render_identifier_index_navigation(&mut output, paths, current_path);
    output.push_str("</main>\n</body>\n</html>\n");
    output
}

fn render_identifier_chapter_group(
    output: &mut String,
    chapter: &Chapter,
    chapter_path: &Path,
    locations: &[&LocatedIdentifierLocation],
    current_path: &Path,
) {
    let metadata = chapter
        .book
        .as_ref()
        .expect("a loaded book chapter has book metadata");
    output.push_str(
        "<div class=\"identifier-chapter-group\"><a class=\"identifier-chapter\" href=\"",
    );
    push_escaped_attribute(output, &relative_url(current_path, chapter_path));
    output.push_str("\">");
    push_escaped_text(output, &chapter.number());
    output.push_str(". ");
    push_escaped_text(output, &metadata.navigation_label);
    output.push_str("</a>: ");

    for (position, occurrence) in locations.iter().enumerate() {
        if position > 0 {
            output.push_str(", ");
        }
        let location = &occurrence.location;
        output.push_str("<a");
        if occurrence.role == IdentifierRole::Definition {
            output.push_str(" class=\"identifier-definition\"");
        }
        output.push_str(" href=\"");
        push_escaped_attribute(output, &relative_url(current_path, chapter_path));
        output.push('#');
        push_escaped_attribute(output, &location.anchor());
        output.push_str("\" aria-label=\"");
        if occurrence.role == IdentifierRole::Definition {
            output.push_str("definition in ");
        }
        output.push_str("chapter ");
        push_escaped_attribute(output, &chapter.number());
        output.push_str(", section ");
        let section = location.section.to_string();
        push_escaped_attribute(output, &section);
        output.push_str("\">");
        push_escaped_text(output, &section);
        output.push_str("</a>");
    }
    output.push_str("</div>\n");
}

fn render_identifier_index_navigation(
    output: &mut String,
    paths: &BookHtmlPaths,
    current_path: &Path,
) {
    output.push_str("<nav class=\"book-navigation\" aria-label=\"Book navigation\">\n");
    render_contents_navigation_link(output, current_path, &paths.contents);
    output.push_str("</nav>\n");
}
---

@s Linking Contents to the Identifier Index

The index is not a chapter, so it does not enter the numbered contents list.
A separate navigation landmark on the contents page links to it. Chapter
navigation adds the same destination beside `Contents`, using the relative
URL appropriate for each chapter directory. The identifier page itself links
back to the contents page.

--- Render the contents identifier-index link
fn render_contents_index_link(
    output: &mut String,
    contents_path: &Path,
    index_path: &Path,
) {
    output.push_str(
        "<nav class=\"book-index-navigation\" aria-label=\"Book indexes\">\n<a href=\"",
    );
    push_escaped_attribute(output, &relative_url(contents_path, index_path));
    output.push_str("\">Identifier Index</a>\n</nav>\n");
}
---

@s Choosing the Book Identifier-Index Path

Only a nonempty located identifier index requests this page. `Greetings`
therefore returned `None` before reserving any path. `--no-index` has the same
result.

For an indexed book, the page sits beside the contents page under the fixed
name `index-identifiers.html`. If the contents or a chapter already uses that
path, Weaver reports a collision instead of proposing two different files at
one destination. The check belongs here because the conflict arises from
Weaver's optional page, before Output examines the combined tangle and weave
plans.

--- Choose the book identifier-index path
fn book_identifier_index_path(
    program: &Program,
    paths: &BookHtmlPaths,
    identifiers: &LocatedIdentifierIndex,
    errors: &mut Vec<WeaveError>,
) -> Option<PathBuf> {
    if identifiers.entries.is_empty() {
        return None;
    }

    let path = paths.contents.with_file_name("index-identifiers.html");
    let collision = if path == paths.contents {
        Some(program.origin.clone())
    } else {
        program.chapters.iter().zip(&paths.chapters).find_map(
            |(chapter, chapter_path)| {
                let metadata = chapter
                    .book
                    .as_ref()
                    .expect("a loaded book chapter has book metadata");
                (*chapter_path == path).then(|| metadata.label_origin.clone())
            },
        )
    };
    if let Some(origin) = collision {
        errors.push(WeaveError {
            origin,
            kind: WeaveErrorKind::IdentifierIndexPathCollision { path },
        });
        None
    } else {
        Some(path)
    }
}
---

@s The Book Pages Ready for Presentation

Book Weaving now returns an ordered group of complete page strings to the
outer planner. `Greetings` contributes its contents page and three chapter
pages. A cross-chapter relationship keeps filesystem paths, relative URLs,
anchors, and visible labels distinct. A Rust book may append one identifier
page, but an empty index appends nothing.

Every page uses the same layout and resolved identities established by Woven
Document. No page has been written to disk. Presentation next explains the
